INT. TELEPHONE EXCHANGE - SWITCHBOARD ROOM - NIGHT (1927)

A long room of mahogany switchboards. Rows of operators in headsets, cords snaking in brass. Gas lamps hiss.

NELL FAIRWEATHER (20s, operator, tight bun) plugs a cord into a jack. At the next board, ADELAIDE PRUITT (40s, supervisor, pince-nez) watches over her shoulder. Behind them, LOUISA COBB (50s, veteran operator, knitting needle in her hair) works two cords at once.

NELL
Number, please.

CALLER (V.O.)
(over the line, crackling)
Mayfair 2290.

NELL
One moment.

She plugs. The line hums. Adelaide leans closer.

ADELAIDE
Dear, who is that?

NELL
A gentleman. He sounds upset.

ADELAIDE
Let me hear.

She takes a spare headset. Listens. Her face changes.

CALLER (V.O.)
(over the line)
Tell them the bank is on fire.

Adelaide rips off the headset.

ADELAIDE
Louisa! Ring the fire brigade!

LOUISA
Already plugged, ma'am!

NELL
^Brigade on the line!

LOUISA
^Brigade on the line!

They speak in perfect unison, startled at themselves, and nearly laugh.

The line crackles. A new voice, the FIRE CAPTAIN (V.O.), booms through the headset.

FIRE CAPTAIN (V.O.)
Engine Four rolling. Thank you, ladies.

Nell lowers her cord. Her hands shake. Adelaide places a firm palm on her shoulder.

ADELAIDE
You did well.

NELL
I only said "number, please."

ADELAIDE
Sometimes that's all it takes.

Louisa unplugs the last cord with a click. The room exhales. A hundred cords sway. Quiet returns.
